# Project: pytest Python Unit Testing Framework

## Framework Structure
- Use pytest as the primary test runner for Python projects
- Organize tests in a dedicated `tests/` directory mirroring the source layout
- Use `conftest.py` files for shared fixtures and hooks at the appropriate scope
- Leverage pytest plugins for extended functionality (pytest-cov, pytest-mock, pytest-asyncio)

## Coding Standards
- Follow PEP 8 and PEP 257 for code and docstring style
- Use Black for code formatting and isort for import ordering
- Enforce type hints throughout the codebase with mypy
- Name test functions clearly: `test_<unit>_<scenario>_<expected_result>`

## Test Organization
- Group related tests in classes only when shared state or fixtures justify it
- Use `pytest.mark` for categorizing tests (unit, integration, slow, smoke)
- Keep test files flat and named `test_<module>.py`
- Separate unit, integration, and end-to-end tests into distinct directories

## Fixtures
- Define fixtures in `conftest.py` at the lowest applicable scope
- Prefer function-scoped fixtures to avoid shared state between tests
- Use `yield` fixtures for setup/teardown instead of `setup_method`
- Document fixture purpose and scope with a short docstring

## Mocking Strategies
- Use `pytest-mock` (the `mocker` fixture) instead of `unittest.mock.patch` decorators
- Patch at the point of use, not at the point of definition
- Use `mocker.spy` to verify calls without replacing behavior
- Clean up all patches automatically via pytest-mock's autouse teardown

## Assertions
- Use plain `assert` statements; pytest rewrites them for readable failure output
- Compare collections with `assert actual == expected` rather than `assertEqual`
- Use `pytest.approx` for floating-point comparisons
- Use `pytest.raises` as a context manager to assert expected exceptions

## Parametrize
- Use `@pytest.mark.parametrize` to eliminate duplicated test logic across inputs
- Provide an `ids` argument or use meaningful parameter values for readable output
- Combine `parametrize` markers on a single test for combinatorial coverage

## Coverage
- Configure `pytest-cov` in `pyproject.toml` or `setup.cfg`
- Set branch coverage requirements (aim for ≥80% on core business logic)
- Use `# pragma: no cover` sparingly and only for genuinely untestable code
- Generate HTML reports locally; upload XML reports to CI coverage services

## Async Testing
- Use `pytest-asyncio` for coroutine-based tests
- Declare `asyncio_mode = "auto"` in config to avoid per-test markers
- Mock external I/O with `asynctest` or `unittest.mock.AsyncMock`
- Test async generators and context managers explicitly

## Test Data Management
- Use factory functions or `pytest-factoryboy` for model/object creation
- Store static fixtures as JSON/YAML files under `tests/fixtures/`
- Avoid hardcoded magic values; use named constants or `pytest.param`
- Generate randomized data with `faker` to surface edge cases

## Performance
- Use `pytest-xdist` (`-n auto`) for parallel test execution across cores
- Mark slow tests with `@pytest.mark.slow` and exclude them from the default run
- Profile test suite duration with `--durations=10` to identify bottlenecks
- Reuse expensive fixtures at session scope when they are truly stateless

## CI/CD Integration
- Run tests with `pytest --tb=short -q` in CI for concise output
- Output JUnit XML with `--junitxml=report.xml` for CI pipeline compatibility
- Fail fast on the first error in CI with `-x`; use full run locally
- Cache the `.pytest_cache` directory between CI runs for faster reruns

## Configuration
- Centralize pytest settings in `pyproject.toml` under `[tool.pytest.ini_options]`
- Set `testpaths`, `python_files`, `python_classes`, and `python_functions` explicitly
- Register custom marks in config to avoid `PytestUnknownMarkWarning`
- Use `filterwarnings` to fail on unexpected deprecation warnings

## Error Handling
- Test both happy path and all documented exception paths
- Assert on exception type and message, not just that an exception was raised
- Use `pytest.warns` to assert expected warnings are emitted
- Avoid bare `except Exception` in test helpers; let exceptions propagate

## Documentation
- Add a brief docstring to each test describing the scenario under test
- Maintain a `tests/README.md` explaining how to run the suite and add new tests
- Document custom marks and fixtures in `conftest.py` module docstrings
- Include example pytest commands for common workflows in the project README

Remember to leverage pytest's powerful plugin ecosystem and native assertion rewriting for expressive, maintainable Python tests.
